 "assumptions": [
  "R2's classifier is NOT re-implemented: grade_v4.py imports `classify` (and apc.ROOT) from experiments/ptr-engine/tools/anchor_prereg_check.py, so grade_v4.py depends on that file (read-only, sha256 c3a17b6c... unchanged). One canonical implementation, per the card.",
  "PROSE-CONNECTOR is decided by occurrence context (tokenize STRING/COMMENT line coverage), per ANCHOR-PREREG.md \u00a71.1: `raising` occurs only in resolve.py:67's docstring, so it is prose even though it occurs in the cited file.",
  "The knowability probe (value_in_prior_context) still consults R1's FULL derived anchor list. This only selects which failure label a failing check gets (refused_while_knowable vs missing_anchor), never pass/fail, so it keeps the taxonomy byte-identical; the required set itself is used for `missing` and for the concluding-position test (prereg \u00a71.2.4 names a `required` anchor).",
  "The truncated-row presence test (`truncated_without_value`) keeps R1's full anchor list: truncation is a separate failure and R2 only trims requiredness, so no flip is manufactured there.",
  "`required_anchors=[]` for NUMBER checkpoints (prereg \u00a74) is recorded but the number path is `number_in()` untouched; PROSE tokens are recorded as informational in each arm's `anchor_census`.",
  "R2 applicability is defined as `marker_required == True` (fixture declares a required answer_marker). Where it is false the arm reports r2_status=not_applicable and is graded by grade_v3's exact code path (need == all_anchors -> judge delegation), so the C grid is inert; `missing_anchor:raising` examples there stay FAIL, not pass.",
  "If a checkpoint's cited source cannot be resolved/classified, the token is kept CODE-SYMBOL (required) and the evidence says so - R2 never silently relaxes an unclassifiable anchor. No such case occurs on these grids."
